WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.030
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.030 --> 00:00:12.110
Today we are going to see how an AI can win
a complex game that it has never seen before.

00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:15.150
Zero prior training on that game.

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:16.650
Yes, really!

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:23.270
Now, before that, for context, have a look
at this related work from 2019, where scientists

00:00:23.270 --> 00:00:29.040
at OpenAI built a super fun hide and seek
game for their AI agents to play.

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:33.020
And, boy, did they do some crazy stuff.

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:39.280
Now, these agents learn from previous experiences,
and to the surprise of no one, for the first

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:44.089
few million rounds, we start out with…pandemonium.

00:00:44.089 --> 00:00:46.929
Everyone just running around aimlessly.

00:00:46.929 --> 00:00:53.850
Then, over time, the hiders learned to lock
out the seekers by blocking the doors off

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:58.309
with these boxes and started winning consistently.

00:00:58.309 --> 00:01:03.959
I think the coolest part about this is that
the map was deliberately designed by the OpenAI

00:01:03.959 --> 00:01:08.920
scientists in a way that the hiders can only
succeed through collaboration.

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:12.430
But then, something happened.

00:01:12.430 --> 00:01:16.240
Did you notice this pointy, doorstop-shaped
object?

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:19.369
Are you thinking what I am thinking?

00:01:19.369 --> 00:01:26.420
Well, probably, and not only that, but later,
the AI also discovered that it can be pushed

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:32.829
near a wall and be used as a ramp, and, tadaa!

00:01:32.829 --> 00:01:33.869
Got’em!

00:01:33.869 --> 00:01:39.230
Then, it was up to the hiders again to invent
something new.

00:01:39.230 --> 00:01:41.679
So, did they do that?

00:01:41.679 --> 00:01:44.299
Can this crazy strategy be defeated?

00:01:44.299 --> 00:01:46.720
Well, check this out.

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:51.420
These resourceful little critters learned
that since there is a little time at the start

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:58.880
of the game when the seekers are frozen, apparently,
during this time, they cannot see them, so

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:04.810
why not just sneak out and steal the ramp,
and lock it away from them.

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:06.950
Absolutely incredible.

00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:10.300
Look at those happy eyes as they are carrying
that ramp.

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:18.850
But today is not 2019, it is 2021, so I wonder
what scientists at the other amazing AI lab,

00:02:18.850 --> 00:02:21.020
DeepMind have been up to.

00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:22.640
Can this paper be topped?

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:29.090
Well, believe it or not, they have managed
to create something that is perhaps even crazier

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:30.090
than this.

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:36.320
This new paper proposes that these AI agents
look at the screen, just like a human would,

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:40.830
and engage in open-ended learning where the
tasks are always changing.

00:02:40.830 --> 00:02:43.030
What does this mean?

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:47.210
Well, it means that these agents are not preparing
for an exam.

00:02:47.210 --> 00:02:48.980
They are preparing for life!

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:55.130
And hence, hopefully they learn more general
concepts, and, as a result, maybe excel at

00:02:55.130 --> 00:02:58.200
a variety of different tasks.

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:03.990
Even better, these scientists at DeepMind
claim that their AI agents not only excel

00:03:03.990 --> 00:03:10.660
at a variety of tasks, but they excel at new
ones they have never seen before!

00:03:10.660 --> 00:03:14.310
Those are big words, so, let’s see the results!

00:03:14.310 --> 00:03:19.201
The red agent here is the hider, and the blue
is the seeker.

00:03:19.201 --> 00:03:25.080
They both understand their roles, the red
agent is running, and the blue is seeking.

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:30.190
Look, its viewing direction is shown with
this lightsaber-looking line pointing at the

00:03:30.190 --> 00:03:31.680
red agent.

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:33.600
No wonder it is running away!

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:40.030
And, look, it manages to get some distance
from the seeker, and finds a new, previously

00:03:40.030 --> 00:03:44.720
unexplored part of the map and hides there.

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:46.120
Excellent.

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:50.190
And you would think that the Star Wars references
end here?

00:03:50.190 --> 00:03:51.230
No!

00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:52.230
Not even close.

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:59.420
Look, in a more advanced variant of the game,
this green seeker lost the two other hiders,

00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:01.180
and what does he do.

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:07.650
Ah yes, of course, grabs his lightsaber, and
takes the high ground.

00:04:07.650 --> 00:04:11.490
Then, it spots the red agent and starts chasing
it.

00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:15.120
All all this without ever having played this
game before.

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:17.630
That is excellent.

00:04:17.630 --> 00:04:23.100
In this cooperative game, the agents are asked
to get as close to the purple pyramid as they

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:24.250
can.

00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:28.260
Of course, to achieve that, they need to build
a ramp.

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:30.400
Which they successfully realize.

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:31.450
Excellent.

00:04:31.450 --> 00:04:33.830
But it gets better!

00:04:33.830 --> 00:04:38.230
Now note that we did not say that the task
is to build a ramp.

00:04:38.230 --> 00:04:42.930
The task is to get as close to the purple
pyramid as we can.

00:04:42.930 --> 00:04:44.890
Does that mean that?

00:04:44.890 --> 00:04:47.380
…Yes, yes it does.

00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:50.460
Great job bending the rules, little AI!

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:55.850
In this game, the agent is asked to stop the
purple ball from touching the red floor.

00:04:55.850 --> 00:05:03.470
At first, it tries its best to block the rolling
of the ball with its body, then, look!

00:05:03.470 --> 00:05:08.680
It realizes that it is much better to just
push it against the wall.

00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:14.440
And it gets even better, look, it learned
that best is to just chuck the ball behind

00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:15.440
this slab.

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:22.470
It is completely right, this needs no further
energy expenditure, and the ball never touches

00:05:22.470 --> 00:05:24.460
the red floor again.

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:25.630
Great!

00:05:25.630 --> 00:05:31.400
And finally, in this King of the Hill game,
the goal is to take the white floor and get

00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:33.810
the other agent out of there.

00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:38.660
As they are playing this game for the first
time, they have no idea where the white floor

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:39.670
is.

00:05:39.670 --> 00:05:46.070
As soon as the blue agent finds it, it stays
there…so far so good.

00:05:46.070 --> 00:05:51.000
But, this is not a cooperative game, we have
an opponent here.

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:52.000
Look!

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:53.000
Boom!

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:58.600
A quite potent opponent indeed who can take
the blue agent out, and, it understands that

00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:02.700
it has to camp in there and defend the region.

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:03.700
Again.

00:06:03.700 --> 00:06:04.700
Awesome!

00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:12.400
So, the goal here is not to be an expert in
one game, but to be a journeyman in many games.

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:17.480
And these agents are working really well at
a variety of games without ever having played

00:06:17.480 --> 00:06:18.480
them.

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:23.840
So, in summary, OpenAI’s agent - expert
in a narrower domain.

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:27.150
DeepMind’s agent - journeyman in a broader
domain.

00:06:27.150 --> 00:06:30.389
Two different kinds of intelligence.

00:06:30.389 --> 00:06:32.780
Both doing amazing things.

00:06:32.780 --> 00:06:33.889
Loving it.

00:06:33.889 --> 00:06:36.290
What a time to be alive!

00:06:36.290 --> 00:06:39.850
Scientists at DeepMind have knocked it out
of the park with this one.

00:06:39.850 --> 00:06:45.580
They have also published AlphaFold this year,
a huge breakthrough that makes an AI predict

00:06:45.580 --> 00:06:46.630
protein structures.

00:06:46.630 --> 00:06:51.330
Now, I saw some of you asking why we didn’t
cover it.

00:06:51.330 --> 00:06:53.600
Is it not an important work?

00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:55.690
Well, quite the opposite!

00:06:55.690 --> 00:07:01.810
I am spellbound by it and I think that paper
is a great gift to humanity, however.

00:07:01.810 --> 00:07:07.680
I try my best to to educate myself on this
topic, however, I don’t feel that I am qualified

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:09.400
to speak about it.

00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:10.550
Not yet anyway.

00:07:10.550 --> 00:07:16.880
So, I think it is best to let the experts
who know more about this take the stage!

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:22.000
This is, of course, bad for views, but no
matter, we are not maximizing views here.

00:07:22.000 --> 00:08:09.290
We are maximizing meaning.

00:08:09.290 --> 00:08:17.910
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

